That’s a theme that shows up in a lot of domains.

Many “games” aren’t announced as games. You first have to recognize that there are incentives, feedback loops, or rules shaping outcomes before you can make deliberate choices within them.

For example:

* In negotiations, recognizing that both sides are optimizing different objectives changes how you communicate.
* In science, recognizing your own biases leads you to design experiments that can prove you wrong.
* In machine learning, recognizing the objective function helps explain why a model behaves the way it does.
* In life, recognizing that habits compound over time changes how you value small daily actions.

The tricky part is distinguishing between seeing a real game and seeing a game where there isn’t one. Pattern recognition is powerful, but it works best when it’s paired with occasional reality checks—asking, “What evidence would change my mind?” That keeps the model of the game adaptive instead of becoming self-sealing.

So I’d phrase the principle this way:

You can’t play a game you don’t realize exists. But recognizing the game is only the first move; learning its actual rules is what determines whether your strategy succeeds.

That’s a useful mindset because it encourages both awareness and testing, rather than assuming the first interpretation is necessarily the correct one.